	<entry>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[cet horrible $$ dans le create function]]></title>
		<link rel="alternate" href="http://forums.postgresql.fr/viewtopic.php?pid=24837#p24837"/>
		<content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>Bonjour,</p><p>Je veux mettre un create function dans un script bash (un désir naturel et légitime, me semble-t-il) :</p><div class="codebox"><pre><code>if [ $# -ne 2 ]
then
        echo &quot;usage : $0 database_name schema_name&quot;
        exit
fi
database_name=$1
schema_name=$2

psql -qAt $database_name &lt;&lt;EOF

set search_path to $schema_name;
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- create db objects
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
drop table if exists audit_history;
create table audit_history (
...


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- generic function for all triggers
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
create or replace function populate_audit_history() returns trigger as $$
begin
...</code></pre></div><p>Mais bien sûr, bash interprét $$ comme étant le pid.</p><p>Et si j&#039;écris </p><div class="codebox"><pre><code>psql -qAt $database_name &lt;&lt;&#039;EOF&#039;</code></pre></div><p>alors là, c&#039;est</p><div class="codebox"><pre><code>set search_path to $schema_name;</code></pre></div><p>qui ne marche plus.</p><p>Quel flip !</p><p>Que faire ?</p><p>Merci pour votre attention</p>]]></content>
